docs: a three-way doc-truth gate, and the two staleness defects it found

A machine check now compares the ROUTER SOURCE against openapi.yaml and
docs/api.md. A route census is only half a contract: proving every path
is in openapi.yaml says nothing about whether api.md describes the same
surface, and neither catches a sentence asserting a control does not
exist. All three were separately true at HEAD.

scripts/docs-truth.py with a thin scripts/docs-truth.sh wrapper. It
exits non-zero only on a HIGH finding — a route in one source and not
another — and reports LOW findings without failing, because the
legitimate classes are known and enumerated: frontend static assets
under /app/, /private, and the /webhooks/gh alias kept for the renamed
/webhooks/{kind}. Every class in that list was found by running the
check and confirming the finding against the code, not assumed.

It is a TEST, not a CI step, because a CI step nobody runs is a step
that rots. three_way_doc_truth_gate_is_present_and_clean asserts the
script exists and is runnable before trusting its result — a test that
passes because the script is missing is the vacuous-guard class this
repo keeps hunting. It also asserts the census is non-empty, so a check
that reads zero routes cannot pass by absence.

RED-PROOFED, twice. Removing a registered route's path item from the
contract makes the gate report 2 HIGHs and exit 1; restoring it returns
to exit 0. A census can read 214 and still check none of them, so a
second pin removes a real path item and asserts the predicate actually
notices.

TWO STALENESS DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED:

  docs/architecture.md — the delivery section undercounted its own
  tables ("two tables — delivery_traces … and delivery_budgets") when
  three exist, and cited schema 1.32.15 when the stamp is 1.32.16.
  1.32.15 created the first two; 1.32.16 added delivery_attestations.
  A reader checking the stamp would land on a migration that does not
  match the running binary.

  docs/CONTACT_CENTER_STANDARDS.md — fourteen rows still marked
  "(planned v1.28.x)" for controls that shipped in v1.28.35 through
  v1.28.41: the complaint lifecycle, the ISO 10004 VoC metrics, the
  consent registry and its no-consent-no-send gate, the 2019/771
  two-year guarantee, the 14-day withdrawal, the aftersales KPI units,
  published code-clause citation, capture-at-close, and complaint
  clusters ranking first in capture priority. The file CONTRADICTED
  ITSELF — line 26 already said complaints shipped in v1.28.37, and
  line 40 already recorded the series exit, while fourteen rows below
  still said planned. Every marker was verified against the code before
  it was advanced, and the one row where part of the claim is still
  open (proactive outreach cohorts, which need an autonomous fetch that
  does not exist by design) keeps its gap and says so.

WHAT THE CHECK FOUND CLEAN, and it is the class most likely to be
wrong: every "there is no X" / "not implemented" claim in a living doc
is still true. BRAIN_BACKUP_RETENTION, automatic N-event checkpoint
emission, no route setting pending_question, budgets stored-not-
enforced, no MCP erase tool, no CLI legal-hold release, pull-only
connectors, three CRM modules. That is the drift class the repo's own
history calls the highest-risk, and it came back clean.

The check deliberately does NOT flag a sealed record — CHANGELOG, the
dated audits and proofs, AGENTS_HISTORY — because those describe the
tree as it was, and rewriting them would falsify history.

Floors: crate tests 2189 to 2191. No schema, migration, or dependency.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
